  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 306 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nThe upper level trough remains just to the west of the region on \nMonday, which will help track a surface low across the region. The \nupper level pattern will also continue to support Pacific moisture \nadvection across the region, which will continue to support rain \nchances throughout the day on Monday into the overnight hours. While \nmost of the region is expected to receive rainfall, the best chances \nfor higher rainfall remains areas east of Highway 83. Latest \nensemble guidance continues to show an 80 to 90 percent chance of \ntotal rainfall exceeding half an inch for areas along and east of \nHighway 83, further building confidence in this possibility. \nHowever, with the low pressure system tracking across the region, \nvery strong winds are expected, with very gusty conditions Monday \nafternoon. Throughout the afternoon, the windiest conditions are \nexpected to be west of Highway 83, where sustained winds of 20 to 35 \nmph are possible, with gusts of 50 to 55 mph possible. By the \nevening, expecting that most of the region will be seeing these \ngusty conditions. \n\nOvernight, a cold front tracks in from the northwest, which will \ncontinue to support precipitation and gusty winds through the night. \nLatest guidance brings the cold front in slightly quicker, which may \nintroduce some cooler overnight lows across portions of western \nNebraska. However, lows are expected to remain above freezing and \ntemperatures aloft are expected to hover right around freezing. This \nshould support mostly liquid precipitation, though cannot rule out \nsome snow mixing in across portions of northwest Nebraska, \nespecially for areas around Pine Ridge. Precipitation is expected to \nfall off as the front passes, with most of the showers out of the \nregion by mid afternoon. However, will still see some very gusty \nwinds again on Tuesday, with sustained northwest winds of 20 to 30 \nmph, gusting 45 to 50 mph.\n\nWednesday onward, expecting a cooler, drier pattern will settle in \nacross the region. Highs each day are only expected to climb into \nthe lower to mid 50s, with lows around freezing. However, by the \nweekend, another upper level trough begins to track in from the \nwest, which may support precipitation chances next weekend. There is \nstill some uncertainty in timing, so will continue to monitor the \nforecast trends for now.",
